4 The rasterizer
****************

The ‘runes/raster’ system, package ‘RUNES/RASTER’, turns glyph outlines
into 8-bit alpha maps (A8: one byte of coverage per pixel).
‘runes/raster-antron’ has the same package and API and rasterizes with
Antron; load one or the other.

   Glyphs are rasterized once per face, size and DPI and kept on the
client.

 -- Variable: *max-glyph-faces*
     The most faces, each at one size and DPI, whose glyphs are kept.
     Past it they are dropped and rasterized again when next used.  The
     default is 256.

 -- Variable: *glyph-images*
     The glyph images kept, per face.

 -- Structure: glyph-image
     One glyph rasterized for one face: an A8 image with rows padded to
     4 bytes, its origin relative to its top left, and its advance.

 -- Function: glyph-image-data image
 -- Function: glyph-image-width image
 -- Function: glyph-image-height image
 -- Function: glyph-image-x image
 -- Function: glyph-image-y image
 -- Function: glyph-image-advance image
 -- Function: glyph-image-stride image
     The pixels, size, origin (X, Y) and advance in pixels of IMAGE, and
     its bytes per row (the width rounded up to 4).

 -- Function: layout-glyphs font string dpi-x dpi-y
     Lay out STRING and rasterize the glyphs not yet rasterized for this
     face.  Return a vector of ‘(GLYPH-INDEX PEN-X IMAGE)’ and the face
     key.

 -- Function: layout-styled-glyphs font string &key (dpi-x 96) (dpi-y
          96) (letter-spacing 0.0) (word-spacing 0.0) kerning-table
     Like ‘layout-glyphs’, with the options of ‘layout-string’.  Return
     ‘(values LAYOUT TOTAL-ADVANCE KEY)’.

 -- Function: text-pixarray target font string
 -- Function: text-pixarray dpi-x dpi-y font string
 -- Function: text-line-pixarray target font string
 -- Function: text-line-pixarray dpi-x dpi-y font string
     Rasterize the whole string into one alpha map, across its bounding
     box or line box.  Return five values: the alpha map, MIN-X, MAX-Y,
     the width and the height.  The bridges draw glyph by glyph instead;
     these are for code that wants one image of a string.

 -- Function: text-pixarray-for-dpi dpi-x dpi-y font string
 -- Function: text-pixarray-for-drawable drawable font string
 -- Function: text-line-pixarray-for-dpi dpi-x dpi-y font string
 -- Function: text-line-pixarray-for-drawable drawable font string
     The two calling forms of ‘text-pixarray’ and ‘text-line-pixarray’
     as separate functions.


