5 The X11 bridge
****************

The ‘runes/xcb’ system, package ‘RUNES/XCB’, draws text on X11 through
XCB (the clxcb binding) and the RENDER extension.  Each glyph is
uploaded once per connection into a RENDER glyph set, and a string is
drawn with one small request naming its glyphs, as Xft does.

* Menu:

* Drawables::                   Windows and pixmaps to draw on.
* Drawing text::                ‘draw-text’ and ‘draw-text-line’.
* X11 caches::                  What is kept per connection.
* RENDER helpers::              Lower-level functions.


File: runes.info,  Node: Drawables,  Next: Drawing text,  Up: X11 bridge

5.1 Drawables
=============

 -- Class: drawable connection id visual screen owned
     The abstract base of ‘window’ and ‘pixmap’: an XID text is drawn
     on, with its connection, so that the two cannot be mismatched.  The
     visual defaults to the screen's root visual.

 -- Class: window
 -- Class: pixmap
     A window or a pixmap that text is drawn on.

   Whoever creates an X resource frees it.  A drawable always frees the
RENDER picture it makes for drawing, and frees the window or pixmap
itself only when made with ‘:owned t’:

     (make-instance 'runes/xcb:window :connection c :id win)
         ;; frees only its picture: the window is the application's
     (make-instance 'runes/xcb:pixmap :connection c :id pm :owned t)
         ;; also frees the pixmap

 -- Generic Function: destroy-drawable drawable
     Free DRAWABLE's picture, and the window or pixmap too if DRAWABLE
     owns it.  Can be called more than once.

 -- Function: release-drawable drawable
     Free the picture and cancel the finalizer, but leave the window or
     pixmap alone, owned or not; DRAWABLE no longer owns it afterwards.

   Every drawable has a finalizer as a safety net.  It does not write to
the X connection, which belongs to the application's thread: it queues
the free requests instead.

 -- Function: flush-deferred-frees connection
     Send the free requests queued by finalizers for CONNECTION.  Call
     it from the thread that owns the connection.  ‘draw-text’,
     ‘draw-text-line’ and ‘destroy-drawable’ call it; a program that
     only measures text and abandons drawables should call it itself.

 -- Accessor: drawable-connection drawable
 -- Accessor: drawable-id drawable
 -- Accessor: drawable-visual drawable
 -- Accessor: drawable-owned-p drawable
 -- Generic Function: drawable-screen drawable
     The drawable's connection, XID, visual, ownership and screen.

 -- Generic Function: dpi-x drawable
 -- Generic Function: dpi-y drawable
 -- Function: screen-dpi screen
     The resolution of a drawable or a screen, from the screen's size in
     pixels and millimetres.


File: runes.info,  Node: Drawing text,  Next: X11 caches,  Prev: Drawables,  Up: X11 bridge

5.2 Drawing text
================

 -- Function: draw-text drawable font string x y &key (colour #xFFFFFF)
 -- Function: draw-text-line drawable font string x y &key (colour
          #xFFFFFF)
     Draw STRING with its baseline at (X, Y).  The underline,
     strikethrough and overline span the bounding box for ‘draw-text’
     and the line box for ‘draw-text-line’, which is what lines laid out
     one under another want.  COLOUR is an ‘#x00RRGGBB’ integer.
     Antialiasing follows ‘font-antialias’.

     Each glyph's origin is put on a whole pixel, so a line is never
     more than half a pixel off its measured width, and decorations are
     whole pixels.  Neither function fills a background; draw it first.

 -- Variable: *draw-with-glyph-sets*
     When true, the default, text is drawn through glyph sets.  When
     ‘nil’, the whole string is rasterized and uploaded as one alpha
     mask on every call, as clx-truetype did.


File: runes.info,  Node: X11 caches,  Next: RENDER helpers,  Prev: Drawing text,  Up: X11 bridge

5.3 Caches
==========

A connection keeps at most 64 glyph sets, one per face, size, DPI and
antialiasing, and frees the least recently used.  The tables below are
weak on the connection, so none keeps a connection alive, and the server
frees what they hold when the connection closes.

 -- Variable: *fill-cache*
     Per connection, a table of colour to solid-fill picture.

 -- Variable: *mask-gc-cache*
     Per connection and screen, the depth-8 GC used to upload alpha
     masks.


File: runes.info,  Node: RENDER helpers,  Prev: X11 caches,  Up: X11 bridge

5.4 RENDER helpers
==================

These are used by the drawing functions and exported for programs that
use RENDER themselves.

 -- Constant: +pict-op-over+
     RENDER's Over operator.

 -- Function: pict-formats-reply-for connection
     The QueryPictFormats reply of CONNECTION, queried once and cached
     on the connection.

 -- Function: format-for-visual connection visual-id
 -- Function: a8-format connection
     The PictFormat for a visual, and the A8 PictFormat.  ‘a8-format’
     signals an error if the server has none.

 -- Function: drawable-picture drawable
 -- Function: drawable-picture-for drawable &optional format
     DRAWABLE's RENDER picture: ‘nil’ if not yet made, or made on first
     use with FORMAT or its visual's format.

 -- Function: solid-fill-picture connection colour
     A 1x1 solid-fill picture of COLOUR, cached in ‘*fill-cache*’.

 -- Function: mask-gc-for connection drawable-id &optional root
     A GC for depth-8 drawables, cached per connection and per screen
     (ROOT names the screen by its root window).

 -- Function: put-alpha-mask connection pixmap width height data
          &optional root
     Upload an A8 image to a depth-8 pixmap, padding rows to 4 bytes
     when needed.

 -- Function: put-image-chunked connection format drawable gc width
          height dst-x dst-y left-pad depth data
     PutImage, split by rows to stay within X11's maximum request
     length.


