6 The client-side bridge
************************

The ‘runes/wayland’ system, package ‘RUNES/WAYLAND’, draws text into
pixels the client owns, as Wayland clients do.  It does not talk to a
Wayland compositor: the client passes functions that blend into its own
surface, so it keeps its clipping and fast paths.

 -- Function: draw-text font string x y &key (dpi 96) blend fill
     Draw STRING with its baseline at (X, Y).  For each glyph, BLEND is
     called as ‘(blend LEFT TOP WIDTH HEIGHT DATA STRIDE)’, to blend the
     text colour through DATA, an A8 alpha map with rows STRIDE bytes
     apart.  For each decoration, FILL is called as ‘(fill LEFT TOP
     WIDTH HEIGHT)’.  Decorations span the bounding box.

 -- Function: draw-text-line font string x y &key (dpi 96) blend fill
     Like ‘draw-text’, with decorations across the line box.

 -- Function: draw-text-into-buffer pixels width height font string x y
          colour &key (dpi 96) line-p
     Draw into PIXELS, a vector of WIDTH by HEIGHT ‘#x00RRGGBB’ pixels,
     in COLOUR.  LINE-P spans the decorations across the line box.

 -- Function: blend-alpha-into-buffer pixels width height left top w h
          data stride colour
 -- Function: fill-rectangle-in-buffer pixels width height left top w h
          colour
     The blend and fill that ‘draw-text-into-buffer’ uses, clipped to
     the buffer.

 -- Function: glyph-run font string dpi
     STRING laid out and rasterized: a vector of ‘(PEN-X .
     GLYPH-IMAGE)’, PEN-X in whole pixels from the start of the line.
     Runs are cached per font, string and DPI.

 -- Variable: *max-glyph-runs*
     The most glyph runs kept.  Past it all are dropped.

 -- Function: flush-glyph-runs &optional font
     Forget the glyph runs of FONT, or of every font.
     ‘flush-font-caches’ does this for its font.


