11 Migrating from clx-truetype
******************************

The X11 bridge started as clx-truetype ported from CLX to XCB.
‘doc/design-notes.md’ explains the changes in detail.

   • XCB, not CLX: no ‘xlib’ symbols; everything draws through a clxcb
     connection.

   • ‘window’ and ‘pixmap’ wrap an XID and its connection, and free the
     window or pixmap only when made with ‘:owned t’.  clx-truetype's
     always freed it, even an application's own window.

   • The colour is an argument, ‘:colour #x00RRGGBB’, since XCB cannot
     read a GC's foreground back.

   • Text is drawn through RENDER glyph sets: about 30 times faster for
     a repeated string, over 100 times for a new one, and a few bytes
     per character over ‘ssh -X’.  Glyphs sit on whole pixels, the
     decorations are whole-pixel lines, and ‘draw-text-line’ no longer
     clips the overline.

   • No background fill: draw the background first.

   • Caches are weak hash tables keyed on the connection, and finalizers
     queue their frees for the application's thread.

   • ‘window’ and ‘pixmap’ are exported names: a package that uses both
     this one and CLX or CLIM should shadow them.


